WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.960
Attack surface is the set of exposed services, paths, interfaces,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.460
and configurations that could be misused or fail under pressure.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.200
In network hardening, the goal is to reduce avoidable exposure.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.140
That may mean disabling unused services, removing obsolete firewall rules,

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:23.880
closing unnecessary ports, restricting legacy protocols, retiring unsupported systems,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.100
and making sure public exposure is intentional rather than accidental.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.120
Management interfaces deserve special attention.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:37.100
Router, switch, firewall, hypervisor, wireless, storage, backup,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.440
and appliance administration should not be broadly reachable.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.900
Administrative access should come through controlled paths such as a management network,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.200
VPN, or bastion host, with strong authentication and logging.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:54.780
Default accounts and default passwords should be removed or changed according to approved

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:55.960
procedure,

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.500
and emergency access should be documented rather than improvised.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.880
Safe reduction requires care.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.380
A port or protocol may look unnecessary until a business process, monitoring tool,

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.920
backup job, DNS dependency, or legacy application needs it.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.860
Before changing production, identify the owner, review logs where available,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.960
confirm the intended use, document the change, choose a maintenance window when needed,

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.540
and define rollback.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.760
Hardening that breaks critical traffic without warning can damage trust in the program.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.180
A good rule of thumb is simple: remove what is not needed and protect what must remain.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.280
If an exposure is required, make it explicit.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.180
Scope it to known sources and destinations when possible, monitor it, document the reason,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.840
and review it later.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.700
Attack surface reduction is not about making every rule tiny for its own sake.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.400
It is about making exposure intentional, explainable, and maintainable.
